The Search and Rescue (SAR) Situation Report is used to summarize search and rescue operations.

It provides updated information or status of an isolating event or recovery effort.  When an incident continues for an extended period of time with no resolution or change in situation, the report will be generated every 30 days. A final situation report closes an isolating incident.

The SAR Situation Report will normally contain the following information:
* PR mission information;
* Status (e.g., in progress, completed, on hold);
* Call sign of disabled vehicle, aircraft, ship, etc;
* Type of disabled craft;
* Location of the isolating incident;
* Number of personnel involved;
* Status of personnel involved (e.g., recovered, located, missing);
* Narrative â€” short explanation to enhance information.

Personnel Recovery (PR) Incident Response Checklists are employed by personnel assigned to the Joint Personnel Recovery Center (JPRC) and the Personnel Recovery Coordination Cell (PRCC) upon notification of an isolating incident.  The checklists provide guidance for monitoring, planning and execution of a personnel recovery incident, oriented to the five essential tasks (report, locate, support, recover, reintegrate).

Incident response checklists may include, but are not limited to the following:
* Emergency Location Transmitter (ELT) Notification Checklist;
* Recovery Misison Execution Checklist;
* Intelligence PR Incident Checklist;
* Joint Operations Center (JOC) Drill.

Personnel Recovery Routine Operations Checklists are employed by the Joint Personnel Recovery Center (JPRC) and Personnel Recover Coordination Cell (PRCC) to guide daily activities or for routine activities such as shift changes and reporting.  The checklists assist the JPRC/PRCC in maintaing a high level of readiness.

Checklists may include, but are not limited to:
* JPRC/PRCC chief checklist;
* JPRC/PRCC shift changeover checklists;
* Director changeover checklist;
* PR controller checklist;
* Intelligence checklists.

The Search and Rescue (SAR) Incident Report is used to report any situation which may require a search and rescue effort.

Normally, the parent unit of the isolated person reports the incident, however anyone observing a potential PR incident may initiate the report.  Sometimes this report is referred to as a Personnel Recovery Incident Report (PRIR).

The report should contain as much of the following information as possible, with missing data sent in subsequent SAR Situation Reports as it becomes available.
* Sea, air, or ground event leading to the PR incident;
* Adversary activity, terrain, and weather in the PR incident locale;
* Number of isolated personnel and their status;
* Location of isolated personnel and objective area;
* Unit and component PR resources available;
* Designated areas that may affect the PR mission;
* Communications capability of isolated personnel;
* Date-time group of incident and last sighting and/or contact with isolated personnel;
* Any other evidence that isolated personnel are still alive and evading capture;
* Identification and location of known media outlets (friendly or non-friendly) that could compromise recovery operations.

